course: technology
lesson: 35
title: Phones and how a call travels
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When you ring Grandma, your phone's microphone turns the wobbles of your voice into electric signals, and the phone turns those into numbers. It sends the numbers by invisible radio waves to the nearest phone mast, often a pole on a roof you have walked past a hundred times.
explain: From the mast, the numbers race through cables, across countries and even under oceans, to the mast nearest Grandma, then by radio to her phone, where a tiny speaker cone shakes and turns them back into your voice. All of that takes a fraction of a second.
explain: Each mast looks after a patch of land called a cell, which is why Americans say cell phone. Walk out of one cell and your call is quietly passed to the next mast, like a ball passed along a line of players.
explain: The most important number on any phone is the emergency one: 999 in the UK, 112 in the UK and across Europe. It works even with no credit, and it is only for real emergencies.
fact: In a mobile call, your phone turns your voice into numbers and sends them by radio to the nearest mast, and cables and other masts carry them to the other phone in a fraction of a second.
fact: The first mobile phone call was made in 1973 in New York.
fact: A phone mast has antennas that send and receive radio signals from phones in its area, called a cell, which is why mobiles are called cell phones in America; never climb a mast or its fence.
fact: The signal bars show how strong the radio signal from the nearest mast is, and hills, thick walls and lifts, with their metal walls, can block it.
fact: 5G is a newer way of sending phone data by radio that carries more data faster and uses many more small antennas; at the levels used it is safe, with radio waves like those of Wi-Fi and TV.
fact: A text message is sent to the network, stored and passed to the other phone when it can be reached; the first text, sent in 1992, said Merry Christmas.
fact: Never read or send messages while crossing a road.
fact: In a video call the camera captures your face, the phone squeezes the video into smaller data and sends it over the internet, which is why it can look blurry.
fact: Only video call people you know, and tell an adult if anyone online makes you uncomfortable.
fact: A landline phone sends the call along a wire or fibre to an exchange and on to the other phone, and many countries are switching off old copper phone lines.
fact: The emergency number, 999 in the UK and 112 across Europe and in the UK, connects you to an operator who sends police, fire, ambulance or coastguard; it works without credit, and you must say clearly where you are.
fact: A walkie-talkie sends your voice by radio straight to another walkie-talkie with no masts, and only one person can talk at a time, so people say over when they finish.
fact: A microphone turns sound wobbles into electric signals, and a speaker turns signals back into sound by shaking a little cone; very loud headphones for a long time can damage hearing for life.
exercise: What does a phone send to the nearest mast during a call? | Your voice turned into numbers, by radio waves | Your actual voice, as loud sound travelling through the air | Tiny letters sent along an invisible string to the mast | Flashes of light shone through the window to the mast
exercise: When and where was the first mobile phone call made? | In 1973, in New York | In 1873, in London | In 1999, in Tokyo | In 1950, in Paris
exercise: Why are mobiles called cell phones in America? | Each mast covers an area called a cell | Phones are made from tiny living cells | The first phones were used in prison cells | Phones run on battery cells only
exercise: What do the signal bars on a phone show? | How strong the radio signal from the nearest mast is | How much battery is left | How many messages are waiting for you to read them all | How loud the speaker is
exercise: Why do you often lose signal in a lift? | The metal walls block the radio signal | Lifts move too fast up and down for phones to keep up | Lifts switch phones off on purpose to keep people quiet | The mast cannot see you once you start going up
exercise: What is 5G? | A newer, faster way of sending phone data by radio | A kind of phone battery | A new kind of phone mast fence | A fifth generation of phone cases that are harder to break
exercise: What did the first text message, sent in 1992, say? | Merry Christmas | Hello world | Can you hear me | Happy birthday
exercise: Why can a video call go blurry? | The video is squeezed into smaller data to travel fast | The camera gets dirty every few minutes | The other person has moved too far away from the nearest mast | Phones show blurry pictures to save ink
exercise: What is happening to many old copper landline wires? | Many countries are switching them off | They are being taken down and turned into pylons | They are being made thicker | They are being painted to stop rust
exercise: What is the emergency number in the UK? | 999, and 112 also works | 911 only | 101 only | 123
exercise: Can you call 999 or 112 with no credit on the phone? | Yes, emergency calls work without credit | No, you need at least one pound of credit on it | Only from a landline phone in a house or a phone box | Only during the day, when the operators are at work
exercise: When you call the emergency number, what must you say clearly? | Where you are | Your favourite colour | Your phone's model | What time you went to bed
exercise: Why do people say over on a walkie-talkie? | Only one can talk at a time, so it shows they have finished | It switches the walkie-talkie off | It is the name of the mast | It makes the battery last longer
exercise: Does a walkie-talkie need a phone mast? | No, it sends radio straight to the other walkie-talkie | Yes, it always needs the nearest mast to pass the message on | Yes, it needs a satellite up in space to bounce the signal off | Only when it rains, because rain blocks the radio between them
exercise: What does a phone's speaker do? | Shakes a little cone to turn signals back into sound | Turns the wobbles of sound into numbers to send by radio | Shines light to send your voice | Stores your voice for later
exercise: What can very loud headphones for a long time do? | Damage hearing for life | Make your ears grow | Charge your phone faster | Improve your hearing
test: «just texted while crossing, no biggie» What is the right rule? | Never read or send messages while crossing a road | It is fine if the road is quiet and there are no cars about | It is fine if you walk fast and look up every few seconds | It is fine if the message is short and you type with one hand
test: «my mate says 5G is dangerous rays that make you ill» What is the best correction? | At the levels used it is safe, with radio waves like Wi-Fi and TV | Right, 5G masts are poisonous | Right, but only on Mondays | 5G uses X-rays to send messages
test: «someone on the video call i don't know keeps asking where i live» What should you do? | End the call and tell an adult | Tell them, they seem friendly | Ask them where they live first | Keep chatting and say nothing
test: A friend falls off a wall, cannot move and is hurt badly. You have a phone with no credit. What do you do? | Call 999 or 112 and say clearly where you are | Wait until you can buy some credit at the shop, then call | Send a text to a friend first | Post a photo online
test: «shut up I'm calling 999 cos my brother nicked my crisps» What is right about the emergency number? | It is only for real emergencies | It is fine for any family fight | It is fine if he really nicked them | It is fine after school hours
test: What would happen to a mobile call if all the phone masts in a town were switched off? | Mobiles there could not make or receive calls | Calls would go straight from one phone to the other phone instead | Phones would use the street lights instead | Calls would just be quieter
test: Count them: on a walkie-talkie game, Sam says 3 messages and Lee says 4, each ending with over. How many times is over said? | 7 | 12 | 4 | 3
test: Count them: a street has 2 phone masts and each mast has 3 antennas. How many antennas? | 6 | 5 | 2 | 9
test: In a cartoon, a voice travels down a wire as a little shouting man. What really travels? | Electric signals or numbers, not sound itself | A tiny copy of the person | Air blowing down the cable | The words written on paper
test: «nan's in Australia, call takes ages to get there, like a minute right» What is true? | The call gets there in a fraction of a second | It takes about a minute, because Australia is so far away | It takes about an hour to go all the way round the world | It goes by post under the sea, so it takes about a week
test: Why does a mobile call not cut out when you ride in a car through town? | The call is passed from one mast to the next as you move | The car carries its own mast | The phone stores the call until you stop | The call stays with the very first mast all the way across the town
exam: Identify the principal means by which a mobile telephone communicates with the nearest mast. | Radio waves carrying the voice converted into numerical data | Sound waves transmitted directly through the air to the top of the mast | Light pulses transmitted through the window | Electric current conducted through the ground
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the emergency telephone numbers in the United Kingdom. | Both 999 and 112 connect the caller to an emergency operator, even in the absence of credit | Only 911 connects the caller to an operator | The emergency numbers require a minimum of one pound in credit | The emergency numbers operate solely from landline telephones
exam: Ascertain the reason for which users of a walkie-talkie pronounce the word over. | Only one party may transmit at a time, and the word signals the conclusion of a message | The word deactivates the apparatus | The word identifies the nearest telephone mast | The word conserves the charge of the battery
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the loudspeaker of a telephone? | It reconverts electric signals into sound by the vibration of a small cone | It converts the sound of the voice into numerical data for transmission by radio | It emits light by which the voice is transmitted | It stores the voice for subsequent replay
